{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794137235,"nonce":"Hs3ZVL/NSYCKk9HltHJvNJqxGac=","title":"CPU raster backend behind DrawContext for embedded and low-end devices","message":"Some users asked for a no_std Blinc for embedded and low-end devices. A leaner GPU layer does not help MCU-class targets: there is no Metal, Vulkan or Direct3D. They need a software rasterizer that implements the same drawing interface into a framebuffer, with SPI or framebuffer display output. blinc_core::draw::DrawContext is the existing seam; the GPU renderer would stay as it is and this would be a second implementation.\n\nScope to decide: which of the SDF primitives, text, images, clip and opacity layers a software path must support; dirty-rectangle rendering to keep it cheap; memory budget; glyph raster path without swash or tiny-skia if they need std. Linux-class boards (Raspberry Pi level) can use the GPU path through a native Vulkan or GLES backend instead.\n\nDepends on the no_std issue for the core crates. Parent: the lean GPU stack plan.","files":null}]}